{"correct": false, "corrected": "Nemá auto, jazdí len na bicykli.", "why": "'Nevlastní auto' is Czech-style (and in Slovak 'nevlastný' means 'step-'); in Slovak say 'Nemá auto' or 'Nevlastní auto' is not used for 'doesn't own a car'."}

The parenthetical "(I am a man.)" matters because Slovak past tense forms agree with the speaker's gender. A male speaker says "videl" (masculine past participle), not "videla". Slovak also uses negative concord, so "anyone" becomes "nikoho" with the negated verb.

The translation of "I didn't see anyone" is "Nikoho som nevidel." The parenthetical "I am a man" can also be translated as "Som muž."

{"slovak": "Nikoho som nevidel. (Som muž.)"}
